"""Layer-0 MLP down-projection, component 5606: a weak lexical detector.

The component's activation is spread over thousands of token types with no single
concept behind most of it. What this program captures (weights fit on training rows)
is a small part of it, in five plain groups of tokens, judged on their own strings:
  * a line break after a space or carriage return (" \n", "  \n", "\r") pushes it up;
  * a newline right after a CJK or fullwidth sentence mark (。\n, ，\n) pushes it down
    hard, as does a closing brace before a newline ("}}\n", "}\n");
  * hedging and intensifier words (very, really, however, because, maybe...) push it down;
  * code and file-name tokens (.py, .md, @, ://, \t) push it down a little;
  * a question mark pushes it up a little.
Everything else predicts 0. Each token is judged on its own, so no state is carried.
